'''Smithcombe, Sharpenhoe and Sundon Hills''' is an {{convert|86.1|hectare|acre|adj=on}} [[Site of Special Scientific Interest]] in [[Sharpenhoe]] in [[Bedfordshire]]. Most of it is a [[National Trust]] property comprising Sundon Hills, Moleskin and Markham Hills, Sharpenhoe Clappers and Smithcombe Hills. It is part of the [[Chilterns]] [[Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty]], and Sharpenhoe Clappers is protected as a [[scheduled monument]] (for its prehistoric and medieval features).<ref name=citation>{{cite web|url=http://www.sssi.naturalengland.org.uk/citation/citation_photo/1001483.pdf |title=Smithcombe, Sharpenhoe and Sundon Hills citation|series= Sites of Special Scientific Interest|publisher=Natural England|accessdate= 31 August 2015}}</ref><ref name=map>{{cite web|url=http://magic.defra.gov.uk/MagicMap.aspx?startTopic=Designations&activelayer=sssiIndex&query=HYPERLINK%3D%271001483%27 |title=Map of Smithcombe, Sharpenhoe and Sundon Hills|series= Sites of Special Scientific Interest|publisher=Natural England|accessdate=  31 August 2015}}</ref><ref name=NT>{{cite web|url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/visit/essex-bedfordshire-hertfordshire/sharpenhoe/|title=Sharpenhoe and Sundon Hills|publisher=National Trust|accessdate=  31 August 2015}}</ref>

Much of the site is unimproved chalk grassland with many plants which are now rare, and [[Festuca ovina]] is dominant over large areas. Orchids include [[Herminium monorchis]] and [[Aceras anthropophorum]]. An uncommon rose, [[Rosa stylosa]], is found in scrub areas. There is also beech forest with a ground layer including primroses.<ref name=citation/>

Clappers are [[rabbit warren]]s (from the French "clapier"), and meat, fur and leather from rabbits used to be an important part of the local economy.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.chilternsaonb.org/ccbmaps/674/137/sharpenhoe-clappers.html|title=Sharpenhoe Clappers|publisher=Chilterns Conservation Board|accessdate=  31 August 2015}}</ref> Sharpenhoe Clappers is an [[Iron Age]] [[hill fort]], together with medieval rabbit warrens and associated agricultural earthworks.<ref>{{NHLE|num=1009400|desc=Sharpenhoe Clappers|accessdate=  31 August 2015}}</ref>

There is access to the National Trust property from Sharpenhoe Road.<ref name=NT/>


